[{"location":"dynamic/","page":"Dynamic structs","title":"Dynamic structs","text":"CurrentModule = DynamicStructs","category":"page"},{"location":"dynamic/#Dynamic-structs","page":"Dynamic structs","title":"Dynamic structs","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"dynamic/","page":"Dynamic structs","title":"Dynamic structs","text":"@dynamic\nisdynamictype\nisdynamic","category":"page"},{"location":"dynamic/#DynamicStructs.@dynamic","page":"Dynamic structs","title":"DynamicStructs.@dynamic","text":"@dynamic [mutable] struct ... end\n\nDefine a dynamic struct:\n\nInstances of dynamic structs have dynamic properties that can be added/deleted at runtime. These properties are similar to Any-typed fields of structs in terms of performance.\nFields remain statically typed and accessing them compiles similarly to structs, so performance should not be significantly affected.\nThe macro adds a hidden field for storing dynamic properties with lazy initialization, meaning that the underlying storage is not allocated until the first dynamic property is added.\nThe default constructors of dynamic structs accept keyword arguments for dynamic properties, with a Base.show method that reflects this. These are only present if the block is free of any non-field expressions, such as custom constructors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"},{"location":"dynamic/#DynamicStructs.isdynamictype","page":"Dynamic structs","title":"DynamicStructs.isdynamictype","text":"isdynamictype(T)\n\nCheck if T is a dynamic type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"dynamic/#DynamicStructs.isdynamic","page":"Dynamic structs","title":"DynamicStructs.isdynamic","text":"isdynamic(x)\n\nCheck if x is an instance of a dynamic type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"CurrentModule = DynamicStructs","category":"page"},{"location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"performance/#Static-fields","page":"Performance","title":"Static fields","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"A comparison between accessing static fields of regular and dynamic structs shows that performance impact is minimal, and the LLVM code of accessing fields of a dynamic struct is still \"nice\".","category":"page"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"using DynamicStructs, Chairmarks, InteractiveUtils\n\nstruct A\n    x::Int\nend\n\n@dynamic struct B\n    x::Int\nend\n\na, b = A(1), B(1)\n\nf(arg) = arg.x^2\n\n@b f($a)\n\n@b f($b)\n\n@code_llvm f(a)\n\n@code_llvm f(b)","category":"page"},{"location":"performance/#Dynamic-properties","page":"Performance","title":"Dynamic properties","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"Dynamic structs build on the static fields of regular structs, essentially strapping on an AbstractDict{Symbol,Any}, which gets accessed if the property is not a field. This flexibility makes for a convenient interface, but is also inherently type-unstable, meaning they will perform similarly to a field of unspecified type. The LLVM code generated from dynamic property access is however significantly longer.","category":"page"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"using DynamicStructs, Chairmarks\n\nstruct C\n    x::Int\n    y\nend\n\n@dynamic struct D\n    x::Int\nend\n\nc, d = C(1, 2), D(1, y=2);\n\ng(arg) = arg.y^2;\n\n@b g($c)\n\n@b g($d)","category":"page"},{"location":"performance/#Tips","page":"Performance","title":"Tips","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"performance/#Type-assertions-for-type-stability-and-improved-performance","page":"Performance","title":"Type assertions for type stability and improved performance","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"using DynamicStructs, Chairmarks\n\n@dynamic mutable struct A\n    x::Int\nend\n\nv = [A(i, y=i) for i in 1:100];\n\n@b sum(a.x for a in $v)\n\n@b sum(a.y for a in $v)\n\n@b sum(a.y::Int for a in $v)","category":"page"},{"location":"performance/#Function-barriers-help...-sometimes","page":"Performance","title":"Function barriers help... sometimes","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"We observe that in one scenario, broadcasted addition of short vectors is 2x faster with a function barrier, but 2x slower for longer vectors. This happens also with Any-typed fields of regular structs.","category":"page"},{"location":"performance/","page":"Performance","title":"Performance","text":"using DynamicStructs, Chairmarks\n\n@dynamic struct A end\n\nf(a, b) = a.x .+ a.x;\n\ng(x1, x2) = x1 .+ x2;\n\nf_barrier(a, b) = g(a.x, b.x);\n\na, b = A(x = rand(1)), A(x = rand(1));\n\n@b f($a, $b)\n\n@b f_barrier($a, $b)\n\na, b = a, b = A(x = rand(10000)), A(x = rand(10000));\n\n@b f($a, $b)\n\n@b f_barrier($a, $b)","category":"page"},{"location":"utilities/","page":"Utilities","title":"Utilities","text":"CurrentModule = DynamicStructs","category":"page"},{"location":"utilities/#Utilities","page":"Utilities","title":"Utilities","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"utilities/","page":"Utilities","title":"Utilities","text":"DynamicStructs defines a few functions to simplify working with dynamic properties, as well as the more general case where propertynames(x) != fieldnames(typeof(x)).","category":"page"},{"location":"utilities/","page":"Utilities","title":"Utilities","text":"NoFields\nOnlyFields\nBase.propertynames\npropertyvalues\npropertypairs","category":"page"},{"location":"utilities/#DynamicStructs.NoFields","page":"Utilities","title":"DynamicStructs.NoFields","text":"Insert as second argument to Base.propertynames, propertyvalues, or propertypairs to exclude field names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"utilities/#DynamicStructs.OnlyFields","page":"Utilities","title":"DynamicStructs.OnlyFields","text":"Insert as second argument to Base.propertynames, propertyvalues, or propertypairs to include only field names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"type"},{"location":"utilities/#Base.propertynames","page":"Utilities","title":"Base.propertynames","text":"Base.propertynames(x, ::Type{NoFields}, private=false)\n\nExcludes field names.\n\n\n\n\n\nBase.propertynames(x, ::Type{OnlyFields}, private=false)\n\nIncludes only field names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"utilities/#DynamicStructs.propertyvalues","page":"Utilities","title":"DynamicStructs.propertyvalues","text":"propertyvalues(x, args...)\n\nGet a tuple of the current property values. args is propagated to Base.propertynames.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"utilities/#DynamicStructs.propertypairs","page":"Utilities","title":"DynamicStructs.propertypairs","text":"propertypairs(x, args...)\n\nGet a tuple of the current property names and values. args is propagated to Base.propertynames.\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"function"},{"location":"utilities/#Examples","page":"Utilities","title":"Examples","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"utilities/","page":"Utilities","title":"Utilities","text":"using DynamicStructs\n\n@dynamic struct Spaceship\n    name::String\nend\n\nship = Spaceship(\"Blip-A\"; pressure=28.0)\n\npropertynames(ship, OnlyFields)\n\npropertynames(ship, NoFields)\n\npropertyvalues(ship)\n\npropertyvalues(ship, NoFields)\n\npropertypairs(ship)\n\npropertypairs(ship, OnlyFields)\n\npropertypairs(ship, true) # shows all properties, including private","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"CurrentModule = DynamicStructs","category":"page"},{"location":"#DynamicStructs","page":"Home","title":"DynamicStructs","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Documentation for DynamicStructs.","category":"page"},{"location":"#Installation","page":"Home","title":"Installation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"using Pkg\npkg\"add DynamicStructs\"","category":"page"},{"location":"#Index","page":"Home","title":"Index","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"","category":"page"}]
